#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "bd1d2ef2-9472-5e57-b071-fbbaf80023da" :question/math-academy-id "e-6731" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0.$ Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\nI. $y''+y'+3y = t$\n\nII. $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n\nIII. $y''+y'+3y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$\n\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0.$\n\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "11a44451-5b9f-5892-b321-925b2a393551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6732" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 6\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=3t^2\\,\\textrm{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3 y'' =-9y' -6 y+3t^2.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 3y'+2y = t^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "aa77f4ef-67b6-5041-9a3b-1a36ffd5582d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6733" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 50t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n\nII. $y = R e ^ {- t} \\cos \\left( 2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n\nIII. $y =e^{-t}\\left( A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_c(t)+y_p(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution $y_c(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation $y''+2y' + 5 y = 0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function $y_c(t)$ is can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t)=50t$ is a linear function. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha = 10$ and $\\beta = -4.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t)= 10 t - 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+10t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147379 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/id #uuid "20b53dff-43b9-5256-ab7d-67aff56db408" :question/math-academy-id "e-6734" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "This is an initial value problem. Since the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=0.$ So, we want to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t}, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c(t)$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 1,\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t) = 2e^{-t}$ is an exponential function, and the constant factor in the exponent $(-1)$ is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha  e^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting $y_p$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha=1.$ Therefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) =e^{-t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t} + e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. Substituting $y(0)=0$ gives $A=-1,$ and differentiating $y$ and substituting $y'(0)=0$ gives $B=1.$\n\nSo, our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\cos t + \\sin{t} +e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d36d31-3513-5ca5-8100-812b7912783a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333040} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8444c0-a8f0-5745-ab2b-8746db8e5526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333392} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6207f8-7e2a-5666-bf53-96422217ed44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333588} {:answer/id #uuid "59eaf665-38a3-5635-ab7a-0276be907a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333810} {:answer/id #uuid "d06b3063-bb77-5eb7-b9ae-7f5d926acc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a8444c0-a8f0-5745-ab2b-8746db8e5526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333392} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a7d702-adda-5168-93a6-866f181c2ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31bc7182-8785-59ea-9a66-360f3942567f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64554" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+{e}^{-2t}$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2741a969-ac92-593b-bd63-41c5cb39e9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+15y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333347} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd7e563-29bb-5b64-86a7-d736a73a9e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333593} {:answer/id #uuid "827cf43e-5b0f-5826-8445-b446c50ef603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+15y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334154} {:answer/id #uuid "995757ad-d1dd-55f1-b889-facb9c7b6a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+15y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334344} {:answer/id #uuid "ede896aa-b9cf-541d-b0a8-5094e93b446c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd7e563-29bb-5b64-86a7-d736a73a9e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333593} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c0e3bd2-74e0-56e9-9f19-381bf23a2455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9df2fe93-bc75-52a9-b88c-221b0f80d0b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64555" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-15y+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ffd1e3-f246-5eda-a2b1-a417f1090519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434341} {:answer/id #uuid "b409ad64-6d88-5925-9ac6-e8d1f16d87e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434346} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2e5274-4651-5496-821c-a506d7dde348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434348} {:answer/id #uuid "f159d57a-7df0-5e7d-b493-2c8d678f6ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+14y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434357} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd99911-9453-528e-b05d-88962c3391a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+14y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd2e5274-4651-5496-821c-a506d7dde348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434348} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df2eceb-b4b9-5cc8-a437-11f8d8f29b3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98c54443-aa10-5205-9d6e-97ff4dfc9f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-64556" :question/problem "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=14\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=6\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}-14y+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d69d6c7b-6c0f-5833-b358-024e1b2373a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡1+3)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392865} {:answer/id #uuid "141a0a74-e302-523c-9f8c-bad2145b337b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392866} {:answer/id #uuid "bc71a800-3396-5d41-b999-dc1cc70c1cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-2)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392867} {:answer/id #uuid "d183e142-084c-5c40-a40a-3e43c9bd291f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2+3)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392868} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2b2af7-c2a5-5791-bb97-7ee22ccd83a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3-1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141a0a74-e302-523c-9f8c-bad2145b337b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392866} :answer-field/id #uuid "2118b5c2-d188-5b07-bc58-c6425a8b5a57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d1bc0c7-99f9-59d0-919b-05c0a19b6daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-64572" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=8{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {t}^{2}+\\beta t+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{t}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=1,\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after one second, we substitute into the equation $t=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(1)+2(1)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f41aab3-f2f4-5bf7-976e-0433ba6153b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}-9y=6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434321} {:answer/id #uuid "28c9a6d3-48f4-5ead-a3e4-26a22313a88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+16y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434326} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc58f4-a0f5-5ee9-b39e-8ca44ac7e1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y={e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434327} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4b5dac-8848-5c3d-8255-14ec9a67a346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+25y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434329} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca051f-7e60-5e0c-902f-804346bada68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-16y=4{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc58f4-a0f5-5ee9-b39e-8ca44ac7e1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y={e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434327} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8e210b-6a79-5d2c-9abd-92d31ac86dff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74f88e96-11ac-578d-832b-92585f234521" :question/math-academy-id "q-64594" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y={e}^{2t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b95bd28-2983-56cd-9bcc-bbf3be6b8e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333242} {:answer/id #uuid "2112540c-83b9-5e31-a8e8-64651d567624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-16y=3{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333284} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd5a079-828e-5a58-a7bf-3c10cbf7ffae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+10y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333403} {:answer/id #uuid "a84fb22f-0363-5cc1-8c3c-7163c3e57f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334463} {:answer/id #uuid "ac246792-bb97-5619-97d3-f01f9ff448d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-25y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a84fb22f-0363-5cc1-8c3c-7163c3e57f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334463} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a4a815-26d6-58f2-92a7-843941c349ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1617e3b1-0c82-5e4f-9ee5-ab5a7b9879bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64596" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=4{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61746440-d9e6-5045-bb93-e252521a61b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434334} {:answer/id #uuid "6792e6ec-4ba8-544e-9f7b-9648a4712648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+14y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434336} {:answer/id #uuid "71438c51-3e77-5b6b-8ede-3b96533c54f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434337} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0f1868-76f6-576c-a678-b5319b6fe27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+14y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434344} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a8638e-b592-5c09-bc01-1e03c3ffcd16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71438c51-3e77-5b6b-8ede-3b96533c54f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434337} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a494ee3-9bab-5cd1-a239-f269920de0f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6dc6dadb-3d80-5cdc-9a3e-a6f7a7c19c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-64598" :question/problem "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=14\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-14y+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56cc10ba-89e7-5521-bc2d-573108d183c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(2-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333774} {:answer/id #uuid "5a143907-ceba-56f1-a9b4-520726bc291e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1-\\pi )\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333814} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b9dee-11e2-5aac-aeb4-657f75ba9cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(3+\\pi )\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334392} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3e4293-24eb-5088-b2f2-9fc987990176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334787} {:answer/id #uuid "ec25efeb-5809-5f21-97f2-7ae5aecda325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce3e4293-24eb-5088-b2f2-9fc987990176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334787} :answer-field/id #uuid "e57d283d-d783-5d66-b8cb-ced36172bad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b5683d4-f207-5c86-9f78-566919581fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-64643" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.$ The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ because this is already included in the complimentary function. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is  \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.  \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+(B+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=(1+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =(1+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f52905-5d8b-5d17-8ebd-7067e851cdfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146979} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0a5530-cef9-5e37-8866-e82eee8b62ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146980} {:answer/id #uuid "c97020b2-beeb-5600-97ed-991001d0ffaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146981} {:answer/id #uuid "258b25cf-fe72-53f7-afd8-ea4ef9f234e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146982} {:answer/id #uuid "501a8c4c-591b-547c-aa37-4fe0ea0fe4b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c97020b2-beeb-5600-97ed-991001d0ffaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146981} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d3b07f-4b66-57da-824f-5775d382ccb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9aa670fe-3757-54df-85dc-a7b3def25d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-79461" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{4t}+17\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+17\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02dd969f-3835-50e2-8bfc-d425532d61e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345375} {:answer/id #uuid "90284f75-7093-5853-a1bf-dfd348956afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346606} {:answer/id #uuid "a6006a37-315c-535b-84c5-4aae73a3316f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346800} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5dd56-c7d7-5b38-9d36-b6ef7d0b527a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347087} {:answer/id #uuid "f4150680-d532-528d-904e-713e4f2b8907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e5dd56-c7d7-5b38-9d36-b6ef7d0b527a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347087} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f9a281-44ca-5645-8aea-5eda107eeda2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41288342-236a-5f59-a0ab-b7381746dd7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79575" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=40\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$\n\nII. $y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nIII. $y=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e435eba-7562-5fb0-810c-8dac762b4077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434328} {:answer/id #uuid "74a3f2b4-0ca6-5951-a6dd-e2cbdba2de6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434338} {:answer/id #uuid "897e1658-e2d8-558a-a228-a8db420f366d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434339} {:answer/id #uuid "a16996e7-7d46-5977-a350-af7a4818e178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434343} {:answer/id #uuid "eaea2c9d-00cd-54df-872a-126be49382dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaea2c9d-00cd-54df-872a-126be49382dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434355} :answer-field/id #uuid "00d4fed9-b01e-5182-8093-0bdf0828e1f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f73fcd2-bb5e-5500-9c9a-4e8cac71bdbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79579" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=25t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+5t-2$\n\nII. $y=R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+5t-2$\n\nIII. $y={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+t-5$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a linear function, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =5$ and $\\beta =-2.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=5t-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+5t-2, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+5t-2, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+5t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly I matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d1ddb0-54aa-5cff-b655-e585e1133a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434322} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8d329c-068a-50cc-b2fb-4a2c309c7ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5y={e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434342} {:answer/id #uuid "adc1bc4b-3da5-5003-87de-2b882c60ff13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434345} {:answer/id #uuid "e64a7620-706a-59a9-b470-dac14db53664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434353} {:answer/id #uuid "e8714b3d-76b8-59e5-9de9-3f570e54ed99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-3y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adc1bc4b-3da5-5003-87de-2b882c60ff13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434345} :answer-field/id #uuid "be158214-78ed-5c4f-87a3-fa42be4c6ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19aa360c-f2cb-544d-9a0e-8248f7db7158" :question/math-academy-id "q-94227" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion with damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y={t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022d4245-6ff2-570a-a5d1-209de982ed36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+8y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354674} {:answer/id #uuid "65948148-93b5-522f-adb5-08d74855dc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}-2y={t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355568} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdce21d-40be-5b14-b940-fe2419ec67d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y=1-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356308} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a71987-a9fe-56fe-bc54-607962e29496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+11y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356554} {:answer/id #uuid "db738211-d361-5a0d-860e-1ca27db32400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7a71987-a9fe-56fe-bc54-607962e29496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+11y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356554} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dcb0b4b-3c35-568e-929d-5f743e4ea885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1618a66f-ae17-56b8-a5fd-31bbc1677763" :question/math-academy-id "q-94229" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+11y=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cc4dbf-2cf7-50aa-bbe7-509d47070250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354734} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1b6885-759b-57c4-b9a8-b9f42753444a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+13y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356301} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4b69ce-aeae-5464-a9cd-fb68e74147e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356337} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf21c36-0252-5de0-b48c-c5a157513b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356457} {:answer/id #uuid "e777cb41-fcbb-502e-bf49-fd5ae4af094d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf21c36-0252-5de0-b48c-c5a157513b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356457} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1310cdd-b690-5715-8274-04ab3b8ca773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f86e92b-34ea-56e9-a272-eb80bc579c17" :question/math-academy-id "q-94230" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t>0.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing forced harmonic motion without damping?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nWe can see that the only equation that represents forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60dd2038-a894-57a6-af28-585a167caf9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355523} {:answer/id #uuid "70ba6f37-7912-534e-af4e-354edb9d8929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+18y=6{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355660} {:answer/id #uuid "754922b1-7b42-56db-904e-15ceab6c74fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{2}y=3{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355695} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4365e4-97d4-5eac-b202-4e562e6c8b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6y=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355903} {:answer/id #uuid "c22c70d9-9504-53f5-ab68-407f3b367e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3y={e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60dd2038-a894-57a6-af28-585a167caf9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355523} :answer-field/id #uuid "54732efb-666c-5bf8-9c4f-c02a10e39c3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a91fb75-0041-5c82-8578-4977beec3c1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94232" :question/problem "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ An external force $F(t)=9{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n6{y}^{″}=-18y+9{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1225434f-ee3b-5bcb-a463-c2289b6fbb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354829} {:answer/id #uuid "347d73f4-d32c-5f40-b3b8-3bc248cebbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+4y=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355130} {:answer/id #uuid "3aed3bad-5173-5e43-be0b-6618315c8154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2y=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355195} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd1390d-5c20-5eb7-ba12-10587b4a1690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356723} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd76e0b-97e7-54a6-b48d-c22a7cc5257f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+5y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1225434f-ee3b-5bcb-a463-c2289b6fbb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354829} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcad8b9e-d911-573b-9e0d-630986594286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a59a904-03e2-5485-b6c2-876a20fc36bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94233" :question/problem "A particle of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=16\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n8{y}^{″}=-16{y}^{′}-10y+4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{4}y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25213c17-424a-52a3-9371-0722697b6bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404688} {:answer/id #uuid "fe19165f-f1ce-59b5-81e9-0ecaae91fcfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404689} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9a43fb-7d21-5c00-92a4-f2ea33cb0a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404690} {:answer/id #uuid "928f1857-0069-5902-a97e-67cf179eed13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404691} {:answer/id #uuid "cd83c873-17b3-53db-8554-5059d348276f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd83c873-17b3-53db-8554-5059d348276f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404692} :answer-field/id #uuid "039eefc7-4800-59f4-a9af-2eb142d7c4e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f3b61bf-2d9c-5a4f-b827-01f0e0d4bec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94304" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in centimeters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,\\beta $ are constants to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2},\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf08c7c-2d36-50bb-9e3b-685c8d932615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(2{e}^{-3\\pi }+3)\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434320} {:answer/id #uuid "4a146e1f-9b71-5c45-9fa4-2c70c38fdb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(-3{e}^{-3\\pi }+5\\pi -3)\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434331} {:answer/id #uuid "60d46327-b6cd-5b92-bdea-875e3d390e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(-3{e}^{-3\\pi }+5)\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434333} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c166a1-8f0c-5c90-a1b3-8e6eef325f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=({e}^{-3\\pi }+2\\pi -1)\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434349} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e52108-7b59-574f-bf45-b53e21a84daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(4{e}^{-3\\pi }+3\\pi )\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a146e1f-9b71-5c45-9fa4-2c70c38fdb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(-3{e}^{-3\\pi }+5\\pi -3)\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434331} :answer-field/id #uuid "96c8d71f-a7e0-57bc-ac1f-0d09e375a0bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11e2d0f3-af5a-5091-93cb-e6293ee563a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94316" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=50t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the particle's position after $\\pi $ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =-3,$ $\\beta =5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-3+5t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5t-3.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=3,\\,Q=4.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-3t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5t-3.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-3\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )+5\\pi -3 \\\\  & =(-3{e}^{-3\\pi }+5\\pi -3)\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0246830e-5242-506a-a36d-7e18041dc699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(-2{e}^{-\\pi /3}+3{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434319} {:answer/id #uuid "1a9369f8-1713-511b-9b87-3dc374534713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(3-{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434324} {:answer/id #uuid "22e73a05-a802-5a24-84ff-9321845a62ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(-1+2{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434325} {:answer/id #uuid "417d10b3-1087-524f-b6b3-6b1ff74203e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(-{e}^{-\\pi /3}+2{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434330} {:answer/id #uuid "56209a80-3fbc-5c41-befa-82b2a27f33a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(2{e}^{-\\pi /3}-4{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417d10b3-1087-524f-b6b3-6b1ff74203e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(-{e}^{-\\pi /3}+2{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434330} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9ca86b2-6c28-518e-b626-d2f95aa8e49b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b338c37e-da00-57a7-9b28-e8f59a2d36f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94375" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=10{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $4\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, find the particle's position after $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is an exponential force. The constant factor in the exponent is $1,$ which is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $4\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-2{e}^{-\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2{e}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-\\pi /3}+2{e}^{\\pi /3})\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d59970-014a-5390-ad87-b6c264b278a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434323} {:answer/id #uuid "66babac9-7d04-5021-9b7c-f7e425d4dffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434335} {:answer/id #uuid "c54d927c-6507-5c2b-ae0d-0a803a216556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434347} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a992aa-e636-51ca-9022-36c97ccfa8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434352} {:answer/id #uuid "efa0ae2a-7f08-5952-b7c0-5f4c014cd9c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4a992aa-e636-51ca-9022-36c97ccfa8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434352} :answer-field/id #uuid "73366abb-1505-5c62-a125-9d0726a1fb41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44316634-3540-5552-a378-8f950333c8f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-94384" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+18y=45\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nII. $y=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{3t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nIII. $y={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+18y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +18 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =2.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t, \\\\ y(t) & ={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
